  "referenceSolution": "def merge_sort(values):\n    if len(values) <= 1:\n        return list(values)\n    middle = len(values) // 2\n    left = merge_sort(values[:middle])\n    right = merge_sort(values[middle:])\n    merged = []\n    i = j = 0\n    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n        if left[i] <= right[j]:\n            merged.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            merged.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n    merged.extend(left[i:])\n    merged.extend(right[j:])\n    return merged",
  "solution": {
    "code": "def merge_sort(values):\n    if len(values) <= 1:\n        return list(values)\n    middle = len(values) // 2\n    left = merge_sort(values[:middle])\n    right = merge_sort(values[middle:])\n    merged = []\n    i = j = 0\n    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n        if left[i] <= right[j]:\n            merged.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            merged.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n    merged.extend(left[i:])\n    merged.extend(right[j:])\n    return merged",
    "explanation": "Split the list into smaller halves until each half has at most one item, then merge the sorted halves. The merge step compares the first remaining item from each side and preserves duplicates. This gives predictable O(n log n) work."
  },
